<p>Create these DNS records with your registrar. The target is <strong><%= @dns_cname_target %></strong>. Do not point an A record at a home IP address.</p>

<div class="code-block">
  <strong>Name/Host:</strong> www<br>
  <strong>Type:</strong> CNAME<br>
  <strong>Value/Target:</strong> <%= @dns_cname_target %><br>
  <strong>TTL:</strong> Auto or 300 (5 minutes)
</div>

<div class="code-block">
  <strong>Name/Host:</strong> @<br>
  <strong>Type:</strong> ALIAS or flattened CNAME<br>
  <strong>Value/Target:</strong> <%= @dns_cname_target %><br>
  <strong>TTL:</strong> Auto or 300 (5 minutes)
</div>

<% if @dns_txt_records.present? %>
  <p><strong>Also add these TXT records.</strong> Cloudflare asked for them so it can confirm you control the domain before it issues the certificate.</p>
  <% @dns_txt_records.each do |record| %>
    <div class="code-block">
      <strong>Name/Host:</strong> <%= record[:name] %><br>
      <strong>Type:</strong> TXT<br>
      <strong>Value:</strong> <%= record[:value] %><br>
      <% if record[:hostname].present? %>
        <strong>For:</strong> <%= record[:hostname] %><br>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Registrar does not offer ALIAS or a flattened CNAME?</strong>
  <ul>
    <li>Move the domain's DNS to Cloudflare (the free plan is enough) and add the records above there. Cloudflare flattens a CNAME on @.</li>
    <li>Or keep your registrar and forward the root domain (@) to www, then set only the www CNAME. Visitors who type the bare domain will land on www.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
